```javascript
class DomTraversal {
  constructor(primarySelector, secondarySelector, depthLimit = 10) {
    this.primarySelector = primarySelector;
    this.secondarySelector = secondarySelector || '';
    this.depthLimit = depthLimit;
  }

  async traverse(node = document.body, visitedNodes = new Set()) {
    if (visitedNodes.has(node.id)) return;
    visitedNodes.add(node.id);

    const isPrimaryMatch = node.matches(this.primarySelector);
    const isSecondaryExclusion = node.matches(this.secondarySelector);

    if ((isPrimaryMatch && !isSecondaryExclusion) || depthLimit === 0) {
      this.#handleNode(node);
    }

    for (const child of Array.from(node.children)) {
      await this.traverse(child, visitedNodes);
    }
  }

  #handleNode = async (node) => {
    const externalAttributePromise = node.dataset.asyncAttr
      ? fetch(`https://api.example.com/nodes/${node.id}`)
        .then((res) => res.json())
        .catch(() => null)
      : Promise.resolve(null);

    const [nodeData, attributeData] = await Promise.all([
      this.#getNodeData(node),
      externalAttributePromise,
    ]);

    // Process nodeData and attributeData as needed for your specific use case
  }

  #getNodeData(node) {
    return {
      id: node.id,
      tagName: node.tagName,
      classes: Array.from(node.classList),
      attributes: Array.from(node.attributes),
      innerHTML: node.innerHTML,
      // Add any other properties you need to process
    };
  }
}
```

This code defines a `DomTraversal` class that can traverse the DOM tree recursively, filtering elements based on primary and secondary selectors. The function handles dynamically generated IDs and classes, ensures accurate matching across varying parent contexts, and supports asynchronous data fetching for external element attributes.

The depth limiting mechanism is implemented to prevent stack overflow, considering performance constraints in environments with constrained memory resources. The function can adapt to changes in the DOM structure without reinitializing the entire filtering logic, and it's modularized for easy updates and maintenance.

To use this class, you can create a new instance and call the `traverse` method:

```javascript
const traversal = new DomTraversal('.primary-selector', '.secondary-selector');
traversal.traverse();
