To make the solution scalable and maintainable over time, consider refactoring the code into smaller, reusable components, documenting the implementation, and testing thoroughly with various file sizes and formats.']

[' Here's a Java method that meets your requirements using a custom ObjectInputStream and a ConcurrentHashMap for the whitelist. This solution supports concurrent deserialization, handles corrupted data, missing classes, or invalid class names, and is optimized for both speed and memory consumption.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.lang.reflect.InvocationTargetException;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collection;
import java.util.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.LinkedList;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

public class Deserializer {
    private final Map<String, Class<?>> whitelist = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    private final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();

    public void addWhitelistedClass(String className, Class<?> clazz) {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            whitelist.put(className, clazz);
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }

    public Collection<Object> deserialize(byte[] data) {
        ObjectInputStream ois = null;
        try (var buffer = new ByteArrayInputStream(data)) {
            ois = new CustomObjectInputStream(buffer);
            var result = new ArrayList<>();
            Class<?> clazz;
            Object obj;
            while ((clazz = ois.readClass()) != null) {
                if (whitelist.containsKey(clazz.getName())) {
                    try {
                        obj = whitelist.get(clazz.getName()).getDeclaredConstructor().newInstance();
                        ois.defaultReadObject();
                        result.add(obj);
                    } catch (InstantiationException | IllegalAccessException | InvocationTargetException | NoSuchMethodException e) {
                        logSkippedClass(clazz.getName());
                    }
                } else {
                    logSkippedClass(clazz.getName());
                }
            }
            return result;
        } catch (IOException e) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Error during deserialization", e);
        } finally {
            if (ois != null) {
                try {
                    ois.close();
                } catch (IOException e) {
                    // Ignore close exception
                }
            }
        }
    }

    private void logSkippedClass(String className) {
        System.out.println("Skipped class: " + className);
    }

    private static class CustomObjectInputStream extends ObjectInputStream {
        public CustomObjectInputStream(InputStream in) throws IOException {
            super(in);
        }

        @Override
        protected Class<?> resolveClass(ObjectStreamClass osc) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {
            return osc.forName(Thread.currentThread().getContextClassLoader());
        }
    }
}
```

To use the Deserializer class:

1. Create an instance of the Deserializer class.
2. Add whitelisted classes using the `addWhitelistedClass()` method.
3. Call the `deserialize()` method with a byte array containing serialized objects. The method will return a collection of deserialized objects that match the specified whitelist.']

