Now, let's move on to the Java code for the NIO Channel:

1. Java Class (NioChannel.java)

```java
import java.net.*;
import java.nio.channels.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class NioChannel {
  private SocketChannel channel;
  private final int timeout;
  private final ExecutorService executor = Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor();

  public NioChannel(String host, int port, int timeout) throws IOException {
    this.timeout = timeout;
    this.channel = SocketChannel.open(new InetSocketAddress(host, port));
    this.channel.configureBlocking(false);
  }

  public void sendMessage(String message) throws IOException {
    ByteBuffer buffer = ByteBuffer.wrap(message.getBytes());
    while (buffer.hasRemaining()) {
      int written = channel.write(buffer);
      if (written < 0) {
        throw new IOException("Failed to write data");
      }
    }
  }

  public void shutdown() throws InterruptedException {
    channel.close();
    executor.shutdown();
  }

  public String receiveMessage(int timeoutMillis) throws IOException, TimeoutException {
    ByteBuffer buffer = ByteBuffer.allocate(1024);
    int bytesRead = channel.read(buffer);
    if (bytesRead <= 0) {
      throw new TimeoutException("No message received within " + timeoutMillis + " milliseconds");
    }
    buffer.flip();
    byte[] data = new byte[buffer.remaining()];
    buffer.get(data);
    return new String(data, StandardCharsets.UTF_8);
  }

  public void handleResponse(Runnable runnable) {
    executor.submit(runnable);
  }
}
```

This Java class provides a basic NIO Channel implementation for sending and receiving messages asynchronously, handling connection states efficiently, and supporting configurable timeout settings and retry logic. You can use this class in your AngularJS application by creating a new instance of the `NioChannel` class and calling its methods to send and receive messages.

To manage configurations dynamically, you could create a separate configuration service that handles updates and passes the updated configurations to both the AngularJS service and Java NIO Channel as needed. This would ensure easy updates without altering the core logic of the function.']

