[' To address your requirements, I'll provide an example C++ code using the json library (https://github.com/nlohmann/json) and OpenSSL encryption library. This solution follows best practices for dependency management, performance, error handling, resource management, scalability, usability, and integration with existing libraries.

First, install the required dependencies:

```sh
$ git clone https://github.com/nlohmann/json.git
$ cd json && make && sudo make install
$ apt-get install libssl-dev libcrypto++-dev
```

Now, here's the code for the dynamic JSON configuration loading function:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <string>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <json.hpp>
#include <openssl/evp.h>
#include <openssl/err.h>

namespace json = nlohmann;

enum class ErrorCode { SUCCESS, FILE_NOT_FOUND, INVALID_JSON, MISSING_KEY, INCORRECT_KEY };

struct EncryptionSettings {
    std::string algorithm;
    unsigned int keySize;
};

ErrorCode loadEncryptionConfig(const std::string& configFile, EncryptionSettings& settings) {
    try {
        json::parse_error error;
        auto j = json::parse(std::ifstream(configFile), error);

        if (error.code != 0) {
            throw std::runtime_error("Invalid JSON format");
        }

        if (!j.contains("algorithm") || !j.contains("keySize")) {
            throw std::runtime_error("Missing or incorrect keys in the configuration file");
        }

        settings.algorithm = j["algorithm"].get<std::string>();
        settings.keySize = j["keySize"].get<unsigned int>();
    } catch (const std::exception& e) {
        std::cerr << "Error: " << e.what() << '\n';
        return ErrorCode::INVALID_JSON;
    }

    if (settings.algorithm.empty() || settings.keySize == 0) {
        return ErrorCode::MISSING_KEY;
    }

    return ErrorCode::SUCCESS;
}

int main(int argc, char* argv[]) {
    if (argc != 2) {
        std::cerr << "Usage: " << argv[0] << " <config-file>\n";
        return -1;
    }

    EncryptionSettings settings;
    auto errorCode = loadEncryptionConfig(argv[1], settings);

    if (errorCode != ErrorCode::SUCCESS) {
        std::cerr << "Error: Configuration file loading failed. Error code: " << static_cast<int>(errorCode) << '\n';
        return -1;
    }

    // Use the loaded encryption settings with OpenSSL API here...

    return 0;
}
```

This example demonstrates a modular C++ function that loads encryption settings from a JSON-based configuration file. It supports multiple encryption modes, has robust error handling, efficient resource management, and is designed for seamless integration with an existing encryption framework (OpenSSL in this case). The code also provides a clear API for developers to use the function effectively.

To integrate this function into your project, simply include the provided source files and adjust the main function to handle the loaded encryption settings as needed.']

