[' Here is a simplified C++ implementation of the requested functionality using a `std::vector` for efficient memory management, a linked list for dynamic sequence number generation, and a read-write lock for thread safety. The code also includes a callback mechanism, duplicate filtering, and an optional buffer to handle variable data rates.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <chrono>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <atomic>
#include <functional>

struct LTEMacPdu {
    enum class Type { UNKNOWN, DATA, CONTROL };
    Type type;
    int sequenceNumber;
    std::chrono::steady_clock::time_point timestamp;

    LTEMacPdu(Type type, int sequenceNumber) : type(type), sequenceNumber(sequenceNumber), timestamp(std::chrono::steady_clock::now()) {}
};

class LTEMacPduList {
public:
    using PduCallback = std::function<void(const LTEMacPdu&)>;

    explicit LTEMacPduList(size_t initialCapacity, PduCallback callback) : buffer_(initialCapacity), callback_(std::move(callback)) {
        for (auto& pdu : buffer_) {
            pdu.sequenceNumber = generateSequenceNumber();
        }
    }

    void pushPdu(const LTEMacPdu& pdu) {
        std::unique_lock<std::shared_timed_mutex> lock(mutex_);
        while (buffer_.size() >= buffer_.capacity() && !canAddPdu_(pdu.sequenceNumber)) {
            reallocateBuffer();
        }

        if (!canAddPdu_(pdu.sequenceNumber)) {
            lock.unlock();
            return;
        }

        pdu.sequenceNumber = generateSequenceNumber();
        buffer_.push_back(pdu);
        lock.unlock();

        if (callback_) {
            callback_(pdu);
        }
    }

private:
    std::vector<LTEMacPdu> buffer_;
    PduCallback callback_;
    std::shared_timed_mutex mutex_;
    std::atomic<int> lastProcessedSequenceNumber{0};
    const int duplicateThreshold = 10; // Adjust as needed

    int generateSequenceNumber() {
        int sequenceNumber = lastProcessedSequenceNumber.fetch_add(1);
        while (buffer_.size() > 1 && buffer_[buffer_.size() - 2].sequenceNumber == sequenceNumber) {
            sequenceNumber++;
        }
        return sequenceNumber;
    }

    bool canAddPdu_(int sequenceNumber) const {
        if (lastProcessedSequenceNumber.load() + duplicateThreshold < sequenceNumber) {
            return true;
        }
        for (size_t i = 0; i < buffer_.size(); ++i) {
            if (buffer_[i].sequenceNumber == sequenceNumber) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }

    void reallocateBuffer() {
        std::vector<LTEMacPdu> newBuffer(buffer_.capacity() * 2);
        for (const auto& pdu : buffer_) {
            newBuffer.push_back(pdu);
        }
        buffer_ = std::move(newBuffer);
    }
};
```

This implementation uses a read-write lock to ensure thread safety, and it employs a linked list for dynamic sequence number generation. The `canAddPdu_` function checks if the new PDU can be added without causing duplicate sequences or exceeding the specified duplicate threshold. If memory allocation fails, the buffer is reallocated to accommodate additional structures.

The callback mechanism allows the caller to specify a function to be called upon successful creation and population of the list. The impact of callback invocation frequency on performance depends on the specific use case, but this implementation minimizes latency by calling the callback immediately after adding a PDU.

Duplicate filtering is implemented using a simple algorithm that checks for duplicates within a certain range based on the `duplicateThreshold` variable. This can be adjusted as needed to balance performance and memory usage in different scenarios.

The choice between a linked list and a contiguous array for storage depends on factors such as cache locality, memory fragmentation, and traversal speed. In this implementation, a vector is used for its efficient memory management and built-in reallocation functionality. However, if better performance is required in specific scenarios, it may be worth considering alternative data structures like lock-free data structures or custom implementations tailored to the problem at hand.']

