```java
import org.json.*;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.*;
import java.io.*;
import java.lang.reflect.Type;
import java.util.*;

public class JsonProcessor {
    private static final ObjectMapper objectMapper = new ObjectMapper();
    private static final Set<Class<?>> authorizedClasses = new HashSet<>();
    private static final SchemaValidator schemaValidator = new SchemaValidator();

    public static <T> T processJson(byte[] jsonData, Class<T> clazz) {
        try {
            if (!authorizedClasses.contains(clazz)) {
                System.out.println("Unauthorized class: " + clazz.getName());
            }

            JsonReader jsonReader = new JsonReader(new InputStreamReader(new ByteArrayInputStream(jsonData)));
            jsonReader.setLenient(false); // Disallow malformed JSON

            if (!schemaValidator.validate(jsonReader, getJsonSchemaFor(clazz))) {
                System.out.println("Invalid schema for class: " + clazz.getName());
                return null;
            }

            JsonToken token = jsonReader.peek();
            if (token == JsonToken.START_OBJECT) {
                return objectMapper.readValue(jsonReader, clazz);
            } else {
                System.out.println("Unexpected JSON structure for class: " + clazz.getName());
                return null;
            }
        } catch (JsonProcessingException | IOException e) {
            System.err.println("Error processing JSON data: " + e.getMessage());
            return null;
        }
    }

    private static JsonSchema getJsonSchemaFor(Class<?> clazz) {
        // Implement a method to generate the JSON schema for a given class
        // This can be done using tools like Jackson Annotation Introspector or manually defining the schema
        // For simplicity, this example assumes that you have predefined JSON schemas in a map
        return jsonSchemas.get(clazz);
    }

    public static void addAuthorizedClass(Class<?> clazz) {
        authorizedClasses.add(clazz);
    }
}
```

In this example, `JsonProcessor` is the main class that processes JSON data. The `processJson` method takes a byte array of serialized JSON data and a target class, deserializes it, validates the JSON structure against the predefined schema, and returns an instance of the specified class if successful. If parsing or validation fails, it logs the issue and continues processing subsequent bytes in the array, if any.

The `addAuthorizedClass` method allows you to dynamically update the list of authorized classes without restarting the application. The example uses a simplified schema validation approach, but you can replace it with more robust solutions like JSON Schema (JSON-Schema.org) or other libraries.

This code does not cover all performance optimizations for handling large input sizes efficiently, minimizing memory usage, and execution time. You may need to further optimize the method based on your specific requirements and constraints.']

