    if (selector.length() > limit) selector += '/';

    traverse(root, [&](auto node, const auto& attributes) {
        if (testCallback(selector, attributes)) {
            unique_ptr<Node> currentNode = move(node);
            result.push_back(move(currentNode));
        }

        if (deep && !result.empty()) return; // Early exit for deep traversal and usability optimization

        for (auto& child : node->children) {
            traverse(child, [&](auto grandChild, const auto& grandAttributes) {
                if (testCallback(selector, grandAttributes)) {
                    unique_ptr<Node> currentNode = move(grandChild);
                    currentNode->parent = move(node);
                    result.push_back(move(currentNode));
                }
            });
        }
    });

    return result;
}

void traverse(unique_ptr<Node>& node, function<void(auto, const auto&)> callback) {
    if (!node) return;

    callback(move(node), node->attributes);

    for (auto& child : node->children) {
        traverse(child, callback);
    }
}
```

This code defines a `Node` struct to represent the AST nodes and two functions: `matchNodes()` and `traverse()`. The `matchNodes()` function takes in the root node, selector string, size limit, test callback, traversal depth flag, and an optional external data source. It uses recursion to traverse the AST, applying the test callback to each node to determine if it matches the selector. If a match is found, the corresponding `Node` is added to the result vector.

The `traverse()` function is used for traversing the AST and calling the provided callback function on each node. It handles potential inconsistencies in the AST structure by gracefully skipping over missing child nodes or circular references.

Memory management is ensured through the use of unique_ptr, which automatically frees dynamically allocated resources when they are no longer needed. The external data source for node metadata fetching can be integrated into the test callback function to enhance selector matching logic without degrading performance under high loads.']

[' Here's a C++ implementation using std::vector and atomic operations for thread-safety. The code uses a custom struct `NfapiCrcIndicationPdu` and a helper function `generateRandomCrcFlag` for generating random CRC flags within a specified range.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <random>
#include <new>
#include <atomic>
#include <memory>

struct NfapiCrcIndicationPdu {
    RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG rxUeInformationTag;
    uint32_t rnti;
    CRC_INDICATION_REL8_TAG crcIndicationRel8Tag;
    // Additional context-specific fields for LTE UE measurements
    std::array<uint8_t, MAX_MEASUREMENTS> measurements;
};

std::vector<NfapiCrcIndicationPdu*> manageMemory(size_t numElements, size_t elementSize) {
    if (numElements == 0 || elementSize == 0) {
        throw std::invalid_argument("Invalid input parameters");
    }

    std::vector<NfapiCrcIndicationPdu*> pduVector;
    pduVector.reserve(numElements);

    auto numBytes = numElements * elementSize;
    auto allocatedMemory = static_cast<char*>(std::operator new(numBytes));

    if (!allocatedMemory) {
        throw std::bad_alloc("Memory allocation failed");
    }

    char* currentPosition = allocatedMemory;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < numElements; ++i) {
        new (currentPosition) NfapiCrcIndicationPdu();
        currentPosition += elementSize;
    }

    std::atomic<size_t> index{0};
    auto getIndex = [&]() -> size_t {
        size_t result;
        do {
            result = index++;
        } while (!index.compare_exchange_weak(result, result + 1));
        return result;
    };

    for (auto& pdu : pduVector) {
        auto index = getIndex();
        auto pdusPosition = allocatedMemory + (index * elementSize);
        new (pdusPosition) NfapiCrcIndicationPdu();
        pdu = static_cast<NfapiCrcIndicationPdu*>(pdusPosition);
    }

    std::mt19937 generator(std::random_device{}());
    std::uniform_int_distribution<> distribution(0, MAX_CRCFLAG - 1);

    for (auto& pdu : pduVector) {
        pdu->crcIndicationRel8Tag.crcFlag = distribution(generator);
    }

    return pduVector;
}
```

