To sort the records by their 'long' offset values in ascending order, you can use the QuickSort algorithm. Here's an implementation of QuickSort:

```c
void quicksort(Record* arr, size_t left, size_t right) {
    if (left < right) {
        size_t pivotIndex = partition(arr, left, right);
        quicksort(arr, left, pivotIndex - 1);
        quicksort(arr, pivotIndex + 1, right);
    }
}

size_t partition(Record* arr, size_t left, size_t right) {
    Record pivot = arr[right];
    size_t i = left;
    for (size_t j = left; j < right; ++j) {
        if (arr[j].value <= pivot.value) {
            std::swap(arr[i], arr[j]);
            ++i;
        }
    }
    std::swap(arr[i], arr[right]);
    return i;
}
```

To integrate this function into an existing data processing pipeline, you can call `generate_unique_records()` and then sort the records using QuickSort before passing them to the pipeline. Make sure to handle any potential memory allocation errors and ensure that the sorted records are passed to the pipeline in a compatible format."]

